r 'I1' through 'I9', 'C1', 's3_doctor'/'ADR 0093', 'force-with-lease', and the residual-discard clause) so each substantive AC has a proving command.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["E6","F1"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.4,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Verification only greps for '^# ADR 0106:', section headers, gen_adr_index/check_adr_numbers, and README index entry","ACs assert the ADR 'proves invariants I1-I9 hold', 'proves its bypass safe under C1-C5', names the residual — none of these strings are grepped"],"finding":"The substantive prose ACs (C1-C5 boundary, I1-I9 proof, per-backend publish-swap, s3_doctor bypass safety, accepted residual) have no proving command beyond the generic ADR-shape grep; the Verification block never greps for I1-I9, C1-C5, the residual clause, or the s3-doctor citation.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb8e2739430347e61","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Verification block vs Acceptance Criteria bullets 4-6","norm_id":"nd40c9ca60b467fcd","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An ADR with the correct headers but missing the I1-I9 proof or the s3-doctor bypass rationale passes all listed verification commands."],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Add `grep -nE 'I[1-9]' `, `grep -n 'C1.*C5\\|C1-C5'`, `grep -n 'ADR 0093\\|s3_doctor'`, `grep -n 'force-with-lease'`, and `grep -n 'discard' docs/adr/0106-*.md` to the Verification block.","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"missing_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a Context/Decision subsection weighing the FORCED remote-override against the non-destructive reconvergence prior art the store already cites (git-bug, git-appraise, jujutsu's merge-of-divergent-operations), and justify why a one-sided discard is chosen over a merge-based reconciliation for a >N-day-drifted clone.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.365,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan Scope: 'a clone whose last convergence is older than N days overrides local with remote rather than reintroducing pre-collapse history by rebase'","docs/concurrency.md line ~746: 'The fix follows the universal peer pattern (git-bug, git-appraise, jujutsu): make recovery non-destructive so the reflog is never load-bearing.'","docs/concurrency.md line ~531: 'both reconverge by MERGE-as-union, never rebase'","Web (jujutsu Operation Log docs, docs.jj-vcs.dev): concurrent/divergent operations across machines are resolved by MERGING divergent operations, not by a forced one-sided override"],"finding":"The ADR proposes a destructive reconvergence path (stale-clone FORCED remote-override that discards a >N-day-drifted clone's divergent local work) that directly contradicts the store's own governing design principle, which docs/concurrency.md states as 'the universal peer pattern (git-bug, git-appraise, jujutsu): make recovery non-destructive' and 'both reconverge by MERGE-as-union, never rebase'. The plan neither cites this prior art nor justifies departing from the non-destructive peer pattern the codebase already adopted.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f3a2fca8b59ea10d7","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Scope paragraph ('stale-clone detection + FORCED remote-override protocol on reconnect ... overrides local with remote') and AC line 3; residual line 'a >N-day-drifted clone's divergent local work is discarded'.","norm_id":"n1fba16d43bdb7bc9","priority":0.335,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer or future maintainer asks why a forced-override discard is acceptable when the store's stated invariant and cited peers (git-bug/git-appraise/jujutsu) deliberately never discard divergent work","The design is later found to have a merge-of-divergent-ops alternative (jujutsu's model) that would preserve the residual it currently accepts"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"In the ADR Context, cite jujutsu's divergent-operation MERGE model and git-bug/git-appraise offline-first merge-as-union, then argue (Rule-of-Three / anti-repackaging) why below-horizon collapse forces a discard those peers avoid — or narrow the residual by reconciling reachable divergent local work rather than discarding it wholesale.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.5,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"medium","irreversible_without_rationale":"medium","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Anchor the §I1 promotion grep to the I1 section and cross-reference (e.g. grep the §I1 heading region for 'ADR 0106' and the absence of 'follow-up').","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.785,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'implemented' is a common word likely already present elsewhere in docs/concurrency.md (substring false-positive, taxonomy item 1)","The AC targets §I1 promotion from 'follow-up' to 'implemented'"],"finding":"The concurrency.md promotion verify command uses a bare 'implemented' pattern that will match any pre-existing occurrence, not the §I1 promotion specifically.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f0d7568b7f82786eb","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Verification: grep -n 'ADR 0106\\|implemented' docs/concurrency.md","norm_id":"n4f5148d85008ee02","priority":0.165,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["docs/concurrency.md already contains the word 'implemented' in an unrelated section; the grep passes without the §I1 promotion having been made."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Use `grep -n -A5 '§I1\\|## I1' docs/concurrency.md | grep -E 'ADR 0106|implemented'` and additionally assert the prior 'follow-up' wording is gone.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In the Decision/Consequences, reference infra/scripts/reclaim_bridge_history.py and reclaim_comment_echo_history.py and state whether the ADR's portable rewrite primitive reuses/generalizes their fast-import filtering or supersedes those one-time tools.","cohort":["A1"],"criteria":["A1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.8583,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["infra/scripts/reclaim_bridge_history.py:5: 'It rewrites an output ref with stock `git fast-export`/`git fast-import` plumbing and never invokes `git push`.'","infra/scripts/reclaim_comment_echo_history.py streams a filtered export into `git fast-import` (lines 173, 595) — the same primitive the ADR names","These are self-described one-time, local-only tools; the ADR defines a general remote-anchored publish-swapping model, so status is reuse-vs-supersede rather than duplication — hence a coaching note, not an NIH assertion"],"finding":"The ADR names the core-git rewrite primitive (git fast-import / mktree+commit-tree) but does not reference the two EXISTING fast-import-based reclamation tools already in the repo (infra/scripts/reclaim_bridge_history.py and reclaim_comment_echo_history.py), which embody the same primitive; the ADR should state whether its portable rewrite primitive reuses/generalizes these or supersedes them, to avoid an inadvertent parallel reimplementation.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7095967efe689e0e","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"AC line: 'The ADR names the history-rewrite primitive as core-git (git fast-import / mktree+commit-tree ...)'","norm_id":"n766f637196a012b0","priority":0.0917,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An implementer building the portable rewrite primitive rebuilds fast-import stream filtering from scratch, unaware of the existing local-only filter (_copy_data_block, filter_export_stream) that could be factored out or referenced"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1667,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"na","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In the ADR, explicitly distinguish the new N-day causal-stability horizon key from the existing `compact.COMPACTION_HORIZON_NS` (different name/namespace + stated rationale for why a separate key is needed).","cohort":["A1"],"criteria":["A1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["docs/config-reference.md:97: `compact.COMPACTION_HORIZON_NS` | int | 1800000000000 | 'Keeps events newer than this nanosecond horizon outside compaction snapshots.'","docs/concurrency.md:483: 'Foldable means older than the compaction horizon' — an established, distinct concept (30-minute default)","The new horizon is semantically different (causal-stability / stale-clone reclamation window, 30 DAYS) so this is disambiguation coaching, not a duplicate-key defect"],"finding":"The ADR introduces a new N-day horizon config key (default 30d) but a config key named 'horizon' already exists — `compact.COMPACTION_HORIZON_NS` (default 30 minutes) — governing SNAPSHOT foldability; the ADR should disambiguate its causal-stability N-day horizon from the existing compaction horizon (distinct name/namespace and rationale) so the new key does not read as a redundant or conflicting second 'horizon'.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f10c4bb30f110e8c0","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Scope: 'the horizon config key (default 30d, portable ...)' and AC line defining the N-day horizon","norm_id":"n86b5b9cb759011b5","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reader or operator confuses the 30-day causal-stability horizon with the 30-minute compaction-fold horizon, or the two keys collide in the `compact.*` namespace"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1667,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"na","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State in the ADR why core-git fast-import/mktree+commit-tree is chosen over the established git history-rewrite prior art (git filter-repo, and git replace/grafts + reflog-expiry GC), grounding the rejection (e.g. dependency posture per project.portability, determinism, or bundle/S3 compatibility) rather than asserting the exclusion bare.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.645,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["AC5: 'core-git (git fast-import / mktree+commit-tree, NO third-party git filter-repo)'","The exclusion is asserted as a requirement but the plan states no reason (portability? dependency posture? correctness?) grounded against what filter-repo provides","git filter-repo is the git-project-endorsed history-rewrite tool; git also offers replace/grafts and reflog-expiry GC as alternatives to a bespoke collapse"],"finding":"The AC names the history-rewrite primitive as core-git (fast-import / mktree+commit-tree) and explicitly forbids third-party git filter-repo, but the plan gives NO justification for rejecting the established, purpose-built prior-art tool (git filter-repo) nor for choosing a hand-rolled collapse over git's own history-rewrite/graft mechanisms (git replace/grafts, reflog-expiry-based GC). A novel hand-rolled rewrite over an existing tool needs an anti-repackaging rationale.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f6a3e118e307dbdf4","impact":0.33,"is_primary":null,"location":"AC line 5 ('names the history-rewrite primitive as core-git ... NO third-party git filter-repo') and Scope ('the portable rewrite primitive and per-backend publish-swap').","norm_id":"n2fd41a87d18d97a3","priority":0.055,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer asks why the well-trodden filter-repo path is rejected and a bespoke fast-import collapse is safer/more portable","The hand-rolled primitive later re-implements a subset of filter-repo semantics, i.e. repackaging"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Add a Decision note: filter-repo is a Python dependency outside the lean-core posture (cf. ADR 0093 R1) and rewrites the whole history rather than a bounded below-horizon collapse; git replace/grafts do not physically reclaim pack size (the plan's PRIMARY lever); therefore fast-import/mktree+commit-tree is the minimal portable primitive that reclaims pack bytes without a third-party dependency.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1667,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"na","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"low","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In the ADR Context, situate the N-day remote-anchored horizon against established horizon/GC-safety prior art (git's ~30-day reflog-expiry window already cited in concurrency.md, jujutsu operation-log GC) and justify the 30d default and remote-reachability safety condition relative to them.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.7,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan opening: 'remote-anchored below-horizon HISTORY reclamation whose PRIMARY lever is commit/tree collapse'","docs/concurrency.md line ~786: 'Git's own ~30-day unreachable-reflog window remains a free backstop' — an existing horizon-GC-safety notion the ADR neither references nor differentiates from","Web (jujutsu docs): operation-log garbage collection and concurrent-op merge are the peer mechanism for pruning history safely across clones"],"finding":"The remote-anchored below-horizon HISTORY reclamation (N-day horizon + remote-reachability + commit/tree collapse for pack reclamation) is a novel pattern for this event-sourced store, but the plan considers no prior art for horizon-based history GC in offline-first distributed VCS/trackers (jujutsu operation-log GC, git's ~30-day reflog-expiry backstop the concurrency doc already names, git-bug/git-appraise pruning). The novelty of a remote-anchored horizon is not justified against these established horizon/GC-safety mechanisms.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7ae798a21dcba398","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Scope ('remote-anchored below-horizon HISTORY reclamation whose PRIMARY lever is commit/tree collapse') and AC line 2 (N-day horizon config key).","norm_id":"ndd393832c69a3b93","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer asks how the N-day remote-anchored horizon relates to git's existing ~30-day reflog window (the plan's default is also 30d) and whether it is coincidental or derived","The remote-reachability-for-N-days safety argument duplicates the reflog-expiry-window reasoning already established for this store"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1667,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unba